ssing in visual search.</article-title><source>Cognit Psychol</source><volume>22</volume><fpage>225</fpage><lpage>271</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2331857</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Palmer1"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Palmer</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Verghese</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Pavel</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>The psychophysics of visual search.</article-title><source>Vision Res</source><volume>40</volume><fpage>1227</fpage><lpage>1268</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10788638</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Verghese1"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Verghese</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Visual search and attention: a signal detection theory approach.</article-title><source>Neuron</source><volume>31</volume><fpage>523</fpage><lpage>535</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11545712</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Eckstein1"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Eckstein</surname><given-names>MP</given-names></name><name><surname>Thomas</surname><given-names>JP</given-names></name><name><surname>Palmer</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Shimozaki</surname><given-names>SS</given-names></name></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>A signal detection model predicts the effects of set size on visual search accuracy for feature, conjunction, triple conjunction, and disjunction displays.</article-title><source>Percept Psychophys</source><volume>62</volume><fpage>425</fpage><lpage>451</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10909235</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Maljkovic1"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Maljkovic</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Nakayama</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><year>1994</year><article-title>Priming of pop-out: I. Role of features.</article-title><source>Mem Cognit</source><volume>22</volume><fpage>657</fpage><lpage>672</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Maljkovic2"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Maljkovic</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Nakayama</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><year>1996</year><article-title>Priming of pop-out: II. The role of position.</article-title><source>Percept Psychophys</source><volume>58</volume><fpage>977</fpage><lpage>991</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8920835</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Nothdurft1"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nothdurft</surname><given-names>HC</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Focal attention in visual search.</article-title><source>Vision Res</source><volume>39</volume><fpage>2305</fpage><lpage>2310</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10367051</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Kim1"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>MS</given-names></name><name><surname>Cave</surname><given-names>KR</given-names></name></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title>Spatial attention in visual search for features and feature conjunctions.</article-title><source>Psychological Science</source><volume>6</volume><fpage>376</fpage><lpage>380</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Kim2"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>MS</given-names></name><name><surname>Cave</surname><given-names>KR</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Perceptual grouping via spatial selection in a focused-attention task.</article-title><source>Vision Res</source><volume>41</volume><fpage>611</fpage><lpage>624</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11226506</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Sperling1"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sperling</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><year>1960</year><article-title>The information available in brief visual presentations.</article-title><source>Psychological Monographs: General and Applied</source><volume>74</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>29</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-He1"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>He</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Cavanagh</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Intriligator</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>1996</year><article-title>Attentional resolution and the locus of visual awareness.</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>383</volume><fpage>334</fpage><lpage>337</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8848045</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Intriligator1"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Intriligator</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Cavanagh</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>The spatial resolution of visual attention.</article-title><source>Cognit Psychol</source><volume>43</volume><fpage>171</fpage><lpage>216</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11689021</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Kim3"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>MS</given-names></name><name><surname>Cave</surname><given-names>KR</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Grouping effects on spatial attention in visual search.</article-title><source>J Gen Psychol</source><volume>126</volume><fpage>326</fpage><lpage>352</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10555864</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Treisman1"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Treisman</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><year>1982</year><article-title>Perceptual grouping and attention in visual search for features and for objects.</article-title><source>J Exp Psychol Hum Percept Perform</source><volume>8</volume><fpage>194</fpage><lpage>214</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6461717</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Egeth1"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Egeth</surname><given-names>HE</given-names></name><name><surname>Virzi</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Garbart</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><year>1984</year><article-title>Searching for conjunctively defined targets.</article-title><source>J Exp Psychol Hum Percept Perform</source><volume>10</volume><fpage>32</fpage><lpage>39</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6242762</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0002219-Duncan2"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Duncan</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title>Target and nontarget grouping in visual search.</article-title><source>Percept Psychophys</source><volume>57</volume><fpage>117</fpage><lpage>120</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7885803</pub-id></c